Cross-sectional inequality in **Population** is tracked across 333 units over 20 years (2001 to 2020).

The Gini index rose from 0.696 to 0.715 between the first and last year.

The Theil index rose from 1.37 to 1.41 between the first and last year.

The coefficient of variation was essentially unchanged at 3.59 between the first and last year.

The log-trend of the Gini index is 0.00143 per year (statistically significant at the 1% level), so inequality by this measure is **widening**.

The log-trend of the Theil index is 0.00129 per year (statistically significant at the 1% level), so inequality by this measure is **widening**.

The log-trend of the coefficient of variation is 7.5e-05 per year (not statistically significant at conventional levels), so the movement is not distinguishable from a flat trend.

On the Lorenz curve of 2020, the half of the units with the lowest values holds 9% of the total (10% in 2001) and the tenth with the highest values holds 65% (62%); with no inequality they would hold 50% and 10%.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
